description:
Why: tests/fixtures/atomics.c translates correctly, but the generated Rust (tests/fixtures.generated/atomics.rs) is far from dense. Clang materializes per-call .atomictmp/atomic-temp allocas that Slate hoists as named locals, producing ~15 shadowed 'let mut _atomictmp'/'let mut atomic_temp' declarations at fn top, with the first store's value (100) fragilely fused into the LAST shadow's initializer by zero_init. Every atomic op re-derives 'unsafe { AtomicI32::from_ptr(std::ptr::addr_of_mut!(a)) }' even though 'a' is a non-escaping local. Results bounce through atomic_temp before reaching the named local. compare_exchange expands to a 4-temp chain (Result match + is_ok + double negation !_v36 + if). Unused stdatomic support items (memory_order enum, atomic_flag struct) are emitted regardless of use.

What to do:
- Fix operand/result temp forwarding for atomic ops in the baseline lowerer (src/lower.rs) so per-call CIR atomic allocas stop becoming hoisted shadowed locals.
- Then recover idiom in facts + fixups: promote non-escaping _Atomic locals to native AtomicN storage with safe method calls, inline single-use atomic result temps, densify the compare_exchange success/expected pattern, prune unused stdatomic support items.
- Extend fixture and fuzzer coverage (explicit orderings, cgen.rs + c.bnf).

Target shape for the fixture body:
    let a = AtomicI32::new(0);
    a.store(100, Ordering::SeqCst);
    let loaded = a.load(Ordering::SeqCst);
    let fa = a.fetch_add(5, Ordering::SeqCst);
    ...
    let ok = match a.compare_exchange(expected, 42, Ordering::SeqCst, Ordering::SeqCst) { Ok(_) => true, Err(v) => { expected = v; false } } as i32;

Rust-AST fixup that rewrites the synthesized `__slate_memchr` byte-scan loop into a slice + iterator form.

Baseline lowering emits `__slate_memchr` (src/lower.rs:~4313) as a hand-rolled index loop:

    fn __slate_memchr(s: *const c_void, c: i32, n: usize) -> *mut c_void {
        let b: u8 = c as u8;
        let bytes: *const u8 = s as *const u8;
        let mut i: usize = 0;
        while i < n {
            if unsafe { *bytes.add(i) } == b { return unsafe { bytes.add(i) } as *mut c_void; }
            i += 1;
        }
        return std::ptr::null_mut();
    }

Idiomatic target using .iter().position():

    fn __slate_memchr(s: *const c_void, c: i32, n: usize) -> *mut c_void {
        let b = c as u8;
        let bytes = s as *const u8;
        let haystack = unsafe { std::slice::from_raw_parts(bytes, n) };
        match haystack.iter().position(|&x| x == b) {
            Some(i) => unsafe { bytes.add(i) } as *mut c_void,
            None => std::ptr::null_mut(),
        }
    }

## Context
Follows the same idiomatization pattern as slate-04q.7/.4/.12. __slate_memchr is a synthesized runtime shim (not user code) built as a FnDef in src/lower.rs; the fixup could recognize the scan-loop-returning-pointer shape, or the helper's body could be emitted idiomatically at the source. n==0 must still return null; from_raw_parts requires bytes be valid for n even when n==0 (a dangling-but-aligned ptr is fine only if n==0 and libc callers pass valid s).
